When lava cools, it shrinks. In a thick, still pool, it cools from the top and bottom inwards. As it shrinks, it cracks. Cracks tend to meet at angles of about 120 degrees, because that releases the stress most evenly. And shapes with 120-degree corners are hexagons.

The same thing happens in drying mud and in cornflour paste. The cracks grow slowly down through the cooling lava, building long columns. The slower the cooling, the bigger and more regular the columns. Bees build [[@honeycomb|hexagons]] for a different reason, but the shape works because it fits together with no gaps, like a [[@tessellations|tessellation]].

{{story:myths-giants}}

The Nile has two main branches. The White Nile begins in the region of Lake Victoria in East Africa, and its farthest headwaters are in Rwanda and Burundi. The Blue Nile starts at Lake Tana in [[@ethiopia|Ethiopia]] and brings most of the water. They meet at Khartoum, in Sudan.

From there the Nile flows north through the desert to Egypt, where nearly everyone lives close to its banks. Without it, there would have been no [[@egypt-nile|ancient Egypt]]. Each year, its [[@floods-why|floods]] once spread rich mud over the fields, until the Aswan High Dam was finished in 1970.

{{story:fractals}}

Most astronauts say floating in a sleeping bag is very comfortable. There is no mattress to press on their back, and nothing aches. Some like to strap their head to a pillow so it doesn't nod around. Others use straps across their body so they feel held, like a blanket.

Floating arms are a common surprise. When the body relaxes, the arms rise in front, like a zombie. Many astronauts tuck them in or hold them with straps. Some astronauts have said they dreamed of floating, even on Earth after coming back.

{{story:body-clock}}

For a long time, nobody could follow terns across the ocean. Then scientists made trackers weighing just over a gram, about the weight of a paper clip, and strapped them to the birds' legs. The trackers recorded light levels, which revealed when the Sun rose and set, and so roughly where each bird was.

The results were a surprise. The terns didn't fly straight. They zigzagged down the Atlantic in a giant S shape, riding winds that blow in great loops over the ocean. Many stopped for about a month in the middle of the North Atlantic to fatten up on fish before going on.

{{story:seasons-why}}

Many modern towers have a thick concrete core in the middle, where the lifts and stairs run. It works like a spine, stiffening the whole building. Columns around the outside help carry the floors. Concrete is very strong when squeezed but cracks when stretched, so steel rods are set inside it. This reinforced concrete combines the best of both materials.

Below the ground, foundations reach down into firm soil or rock. The Burj Khalifa in [[@dubai|Dubai]], 828 metres tall, stands on a thick concrete slab resting on nearly 200 concrete piles. They reach more than 40 metres into the ground, about as deep as a 13-storey building is tall.

{{story:earthquakes}}

The rice flour matters. Ants, sparrows and other small creatures come and eat it. For many families, drawing a kolam is a way of feeding other living things at the start of the day.

Kolams also send messages. A doorstep with no kolam can be a sign that the family is in mourning. During festivals, kolams grow bigger, with colour and flowers added. In the Tamil month of Margazhi, from mid-December to mid-January, whole streets fill with giant designs, and some towns hold kolam competitions. At [[@onam-pongal|Pongal]], designs show pots of rice boiling over.

{{story:onam-pongal}}

For decades, a band recorded all together, live in one room. In old Bombay film studios, singers like [[@lata-mangeshkar|Lata Mangeshkar]] stood at a microphone in front of a whole orchestra. If anyone slipped, everyone started again.

From the late 1940s, the American guitarist Les Paul pioneered recording on top of earlier recordings, called overdubbing. Tape machines that could record several separate tracks followed. In 1967 the Beatles made *Sgt. Pepper's Lonely Hearts Club Band* using four-track tape machines, copying finished tracks together to free up space for more. Today a laptop can hold hundreds of tracks.

{{story:sound-recording}}

Humayun's son [[@akbar|Akbar]] became a great [[patron|patron]] and turned painting into a big project. He set up a workshop, called a *karkhana*, where more than a hundred artists worked. Many were Hindus from Gujarat, Gwalior and elsewhere, working alongside painters with Persian training.

Their most famous task was the *Hamzanama*, the adventures of a hero called Amir Hamza. It had about 1,400 paintings on cloth, each roughly the size of a newspaper page, and took around fifteen years. Akbar, who struggled to read, liked to have stories read aloud while the pictures were held up. Only a small number of these pages survive today, scattered across museums in several countries.

{{story:dodo}}

Another trick helps with numbers close to 100. Say you want 97 × 96. Note how far each is below 100: 3 and 4. Take one of those away from the other number: 97 − 4 = 93. That gives the first two digits. Then multiply the gaps: 3 × 4 = 12. Answer: 9,312.

This method is called *nikhilam* in the book *Vedic Mathematics*, written by Bharati Krishna Tirtha and published in 1965. The book became popular in schools. But historians point out that its sutras aren't in the Vedas. It is a modern book with an old-sounding name. Still, the [[@vedic-maths|Vedic maths]] tricks are a quick, fun way to practise sums in your head.

{{story:mental-maths}}

Translating for dubbing is tricky. A line must mean the same thing, fit the same number of seconds and roughly match the mouth. If a character closes their lips on screen, the new line needs a sound such as "m", "b" or "p" near that moment.

Jokes are the hardest. A [[@words-puns|pun]] in English may make no sense in Marathi, so translators often invent a new joke that gets a laugh at the same moment. Songs may be rewritten completely so they rhyme and fit the tune.

{{story:voice}}

In 2012, the singer PSY released "Gangnam Style", a joke song about a rich [[@seoul|Seoul]] neighbourhood. Its silly horse-riding dance made it the first video on YouTube to pass 1 billion views.

{{story:youtube}}

BTS, a seven-member group, debuted in 2013. They spoke to fans directly through [[@social-media|social media]] and live videos, and their fan club, called ARMY, grew into millions. In 2020 their song "Dynamite" became the first by a K-pop act to top the US Billboard Hot 100. In 2019, Blackpink became the first K-pop girl group to play at the Coachella festival in California.

Researchers at the University of Portsmouth in England found that after just a handful of short, careful dips, people's gasp response became noticeably weaker. Regular cold swimmers often shiver less and feel less panic.

Their blood vessels near the skin squeeze shut quickly, so warm blood stays deep inside. Some may also have more brown fat, a special kind of fat that burns energy to make heat. Babies have lots of it. A layer of ordinary fat under the skin helps too, the way blubber keeps a seal or a [[@penguins-cold|penguin]] warm in polar seas.

{{story:power-wood-frog}}

During REM sleep, your brain sends out signals that block most of your muscles. You can still breathe, and your eyes can move, but your arms and legs go limp. This keeps you from leaping out of bed to chase a dream football.

Sometimes, just as you wake up, the pause lasts a few seconds too long and you can't move. It feels scary but is harmless, and it passes quickly.

{{story:ramanujan}}

You can [[@body-breath-hold|hold your breath]], but not for long, and you never have to remember to breathe while you sleep. A part of your brainstem, at the base of the brain, keeps the [[rhythm|rhythm]] going.

It mostly watches carbon dioxide, not oxygen. When carbon dioxide builds up in your blood, the brainstem sends a stronger signal to the diaphragm. That burning urge you feel when you hold your breath is really your body noticing too much carbon dioxide. When you run, your muscles make more of it, so you breathe faster and deeper without deciding to.

{{story:bar-headed-goose}}

India changed its drone rules in 2021 to make flying easier. In the same year, the Telangana government tested "Medicine from the Sky", flying vaccines and medicines to remote health centres. The Indian Council of Medical Research used drones to carry vaccines across hilly parts of Manipur and Nagaland.

Every drone flight in India is checked against the Digital Sky map. Green zones are open, yellow zones need permission, and red zones, such as near [[@airports|airports]] and borders, are off-limits without special approval. This is a small part of the country's [[@air-traffic|air traffic]] system, which must keep drones away from planes and helicopters.

{{story:air-traffic}}

Tube trains are not a new idea. In 1812 a London inventor named George Medhurst described pushing carriages through pipes with air. In the early 1900s, Robert Goddard, the American rocket pioneer, sketched a train in a [[vacuum|vacuum]] tube.

In 2013 Elon Musk, the head of Tesla and SpaceX, put out a paper with a team of engineers called Hyperloop Alpha. It suggested a tube between Los Angeles and San Francisco and gave the idea its catchy name. Companies sprang up around the world to build one.

{{story:sound-travel}}

At first, officials in cities like Uruk drew small pictures on clay to count grain and animals. Over time, the pictures became groups of wedge-shaped marks pressed in with a cut reed. This is called cuneiform, from the Latin word for "wedge". It was used for about 3,000 years, for many languages.

Hundreds of thousands of tablets survive, because clay lasts, especially when baked. They include shopping lists, school exercises, letters and complaints. In one, a man named Nanni grumbles to a merchant called Ea-nasir about bad copper. It is often called the oldest written customer complaint. That is how [[@writing-began|writing]] began: with business.

{{story:wheel-invented}}

In January 1941, Bose slipped past the police guarding his house, disguised as an insurance agent. He crossed into Afghanistan, then travelled through the Soviet Union to [[@germany|Germany]].

In 1943 he made an even stranger trip. A German submarine carried him south around Africa. Near Madagascar, he climbed into a rubber boat in rough seas and crossed to a Japanese submarine waiting nearby.

{{story:submarines}}

Chariots spread fast. Egypt's pharaohs built huge chariot forces, and so did the Hittites in what is now Turkey. Around 1274 BCE, the two armies met at Kadesh in Syria with thousands of chariots on the field. Pharaoh Ramesses II later carved the battle onto temple walls, claiming a great victory. The Hittite records tell a different story, and historians think it ended in a draw.

A typical chariot had a driver and a fighter with a bow or spears. It rarely smashed into enemy lines. It raced along the edges, its archer shooting, then swung away before anyone could catch it. The young pharaoh Tutankhamun was buried with six chariots. In China, rulers of the Shang [[dynasty|dynasty]] were buried with whole chariots and their horses.

{{story:horses}}

Britain joined the northern and southern parts of the region into a single colony in 1914. Nigeria became independent on 1 October 1960, one of many African countries to win freedom in the wave of [[@decolonisation|decolonisation]] that year.

The years that followed were hard. From 1967 to 1970, a civil war was fought after the south-east tried to break away as Biafra, and a very large number of people died, many from hunger. Since 1999, Nigeria has had elected governments. Oil from the Niger Delta has made the government much of its money, though many Nigerians remain poor.

{{story:bollywood}}

Saffron comes from the crocus flower. Each flower has just three red threads, the stigmas, which must be picked by hand, early in the morning. It takes about 150 flowers for a single gram.

Iran grows most of the world's saffron. India's comes mainly from Pampore in [[@kashmir|Kashmir]], where fields turn purple each autumn. Kashmir saffron got a GI tag in 2020. Because it's so costly, saffron is often faked with dyed corn silk or flower petals. Real saffron colours milk slowly and evenly, while fakes bleed colour fast.

{{story:saffron}}

In the 1950s and 1960s, India was short of milk. Then a young engineer named Verghese Kurien began working with a [[cooperative|cooperative]] of farmers in [[@amul|Anand]], Gujarat. Each farmer owned a few animals and brought milk to a village centre twice a day. The cooperative, later known by its brand name Amul, collected it, tested it and paid fairly.

In 1970, the government spread this model across the country in a programme called Operation Flood. It grew into the [[@white-revolution|White Revolution]], and by the late 1990s India had become the world's biggest milk producer. Kurien's birthday, 26 November, is now National Milk Day.

{{story:white-revolution}}
